{"cuisine/cuisine-of-mantua":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-mantua","n":"Mantuan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Lombard cuisine","cuisine/lombard-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Mantuan cuisine is the set of traditional dishes of the Italian province of Mantua, some of which date back to the time of the Gonzaga."],["It is a cuisine bound to the land by peasant traditions; however, it is very rich and varied. Differences can be found between local variants of the same dish."],["Given the geographical position occupied by the province of Mantua, the Mantuan culinary tradition is similar to Emilian cuisine, where salami and pasta are more prevalent  and to Lombard cuisine, which makes more use of rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-mantua"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Mantua"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Lombard cuisine","cuisine/lombard-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Lombard cuisine","cuisine/lombard-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ee/Agnolini_mantovani.jpg/500px-Agnolini_mantovani.jpg","w":500,"h":496,"by":"Teseo","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agnolini_mantovani.jpg"}},"cuisine/castilian-leonese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/castilian-leonese-cuisine","n":"Castilian-Leonese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Spain","intro":[["Castilian-Leonese cuisine refers to the typical dishes and ingredients of the region of Castile and León in Spain. This cuisine is known for its cooked dishes (guiso) and its grilled or roasted meats (asado), its high-quality wines, the variety of its desserts, its sausages (embutidos), and its cheeses."],["In addition, in certain areas of Castile and León, one can find the important production of apples, almond paste, and more."],["Castilian-Leonese cuisine is built around stews and asados, as well as a large assortment of desserts. The major dishes in this cuisine are of veal, morcillas, legumes (such as green beans, chickpeas, and lentils), simple soups with garlic, and select wines. Other major dishes include pork and embutidos, found all over Castile and León, but that reach their peak in Salamanca (specifically in Guijuelo and Candelario); several types of empanadas; roast lamb and suckling pig; morcilla; haricots; cocido maragato (eaten in reverse order); botillo of Bierzo; jamón from Guijuelo; and queso castellano (a sheep's-milk cheese)."],["Major wines in Castilian-Leonese cuisine include the robust wine of Toro, reds from Ribera del Duero, whites from Rueda, and clarets from Cigales."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"castilian-leonese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castilian-Leonese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9b/Cochinillo_en_%C3%81vila.jpg/500px-Cochinillo_en_%C3%81vila.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Xemenendura","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cochinillo_en_%C3%81vila.jpg"}},"cuisine/history-of-indian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/history-of-indian-cuisine","n":"Historical Indian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Historical cuisine in India","intro":[["The history of Indian cuisine consists of cuisine of the Indian subcontinent, which is rich and diverse. The diverse climate in the region, ranging from deep tropical to alpine, has also helped considerably broaden the set of ingredients readily available to the many schools of cookery in India. In many cases, food has become a marker of religious and social identity, with varying taboos and preferences which has also driven these groups to innovate extensively with the food sources that are deemed acceptable."],["One strong influence over Indian foods is the longstanding vegetarianism within sections of Hindu and Jain communities. At 31%, slightly less than a third of Indians are vegetarians."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"history-of-indian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Indian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The Mughal Feast: Recipes from the Kitchen of Emperor Shah Jahan","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Salma Yusuf Husain","note":"Cookable adaptation of the Persian-language Mughal manuscript Nuskha-e-Shahjahani by food historian Salma Husain.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"The Emperor's Table: The Art of Mughal Cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Salma Husain","note":"Salma Husain's collection of Mughal court-style recipes.","url":"","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"dish/ambrosia-fruit-salad":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ambrosia-fruit-salad","n":"Ambrosia (fruit salad)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Southern American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Ambrosia also known as ambrosia salad is a North American variety of fruit salad originating in the Southern United States. Most ambrosia recipes contain canned (often sweetened) or fresh pineapple, canned mandarin orange slices or fresh orange sections, miniature marshmallows, and coconut. Other ingredients might include various fruits and nuts: maraschino cherries, bananas, strawberries, peeled grapes, or crushed pecans. Ambrosia can also include mayonnaise or dairy ingredients: whipped cream (or whipped topping), sour cream, cream cheese, pudding, yogurt, or cottage cheese."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ambrosia-fruit-salad"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambrosia_(fruit_salad)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Mandarin orange","ingredient/mandarin-orange"],["Marshmallow","ingredient/marshmallow"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Lana's Cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lanascooking.com","note":"Lana Stuart's blog of Southern home cooking from Georgia.","url":"https://www.lanascooking.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"A Southern Soul: Southern Classics","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"asouthernsoul.com","note":"Collection of classic Southern recipes on a Southern home cooking blog.","url":"https://asouthernsoul.com/recipes/southern-classics","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Southern American cuisine","links":[["Barbecue bologna","dish/barbecue-bologna"],["Chess pie","dish/chess-pie"],["Chicken Maryland","dish/chicken-maryland"],["Chitlins","dish/chitlins"],["Conecuh Sausage","dish/conecuh-sausage"],["Grape hull pie","dish/grape-hull-pie"],["Hash (stew)","dish/hash-stew"],["Lemon pepper wings","dish/lemon-pepper-wings"],["Mississippi mud pie","dish/mississippi-mud-pie"],["Monroe Sausage","dish/monroe-sausage"],["Moon Pie","dish/moon-pie"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3c/Ambrosia_salad.jpg/500px-Ambrosia_salad.jpg","w":500,"h":426,"by":"Marshall Astor","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ambrosia_salad.jpg"}},"ingredient/coconut-oil":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/coconut-oil","n":"Coconut oil","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Coconut oil (or coconut fat) is an edible oil derived from the meat of the coconut palm fruit. Coconut oil is a white solid fat below around 25 °C (77 °F), and a clear thin liquid oil at higher temperatures. Unrefined varieties have a distinct coconut aroma. Coconut oil is used as a food oil, and in industrial applications for cosmetics and detergent production. The oil is rich in medium-chain fatty acids."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-coconut-oil"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coconut_oil"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Coconut water","ingredient/coconut-water"],["Deep fried","ingredient/deep-fried"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Shallots","ingredient/shallots"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f3/Coconut_and_oil.jpg/500px-Coconut_and_oil.jpg","w":500,"h":543,"by":"Phu Thinh Co","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coconut_and_oil.jpg"}},"ingredient/iru":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/iru","n":"Iru","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Irú (Yoruba) or Daddawa (Hausa) or Eware (Edo) or Sumbala (Bambara) or Narghi (Fula) or Zwuaii (Tyap) is a type of fermented and processed locust beans (Parkia biglobosa) or soybean used as a condiment in cooking. It is similar to ogiri and douchi.  It is popular throughout West African cuisine. It is used in cooking traditional soups like egusi soup, okro soup (ila), Ewedu soup, and ogbono soup."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-iru"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iru_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",[["Ofada rice","dish/ofada-rice"]]],["Yoruba cuisine","cuisine/yoruba-cuisine",[["Draw soup","dish/draw-soup"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"],["Ewedu","ingredient/ewedu"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Ogbono","ingredient/ogbono"],["Okra","ingredient/okra"],["Palm oil","ingredient/palm-oil"]]}]},"dish/foie-gras":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/foie-gras","n":"Foie gras","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Foie gras is a specialty food product made of the liver of a duck or goose. According to French law, foie gras is defined as the liver of a duck or goose fattened by gavage (force feeding)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-foie-gras"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foie_gras"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Domestic duck","ingredient/domestic-duck"],["Domestic goose","ingredient/domestic-goose"],["Liver","ingredient/liver"],["Liver as food","ingredient/liver-as-food"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"nam